<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml v="1" site="pinwheelexplained.com" epoch="1">
  <head>
    <title>Pinwheel, explained</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A sealed site on the Kickl network, with the actual point written down."/>
    <meta name="headline" content="A sealed site on Kickl's own network, with the actual point written down."/>
    <meta name="tags" content="kickl, privacy, sealed-pages, pinwheel, registry"/>
  </head>
  <body>
    <page>
      <h1>Pinwheel, explained</h1>
      <badge>sealed document · no scripts · no trackers</badge>

      <p>This page lives at <b>jttp://pinwheelexplained.com</b>. That address is not a
      website on the open internet. It is a name in Kickl's own phonebook, served from a
      sealed document that this browser authenticates before it renders a single word.</p>

      <hr/>

      <h2>What "sealed" means here</h2>
      <p>The page you are reading is plain structure. The things worth hiding are blobs:
      notes, files, keys, mail, chat. Each blob is encrypted with <b>AES-256-GCM</b> under a
      document key, and that document key is wrapped for the reader. Without the user key,
      a blob is just noise with a tag that will not open.</p>

      <p>There is no JavaScript on this surface. Not "disabled by a setting" — absent from the
      format. A KML document cannot run code, read the clipboard, query your device, or phone
      home, because the renderer has no instruction that does those things.</p>

      <blob id="b1" mime="text/plain"/>

      <h2>The cipher rules</h2>
      <p>One symmetric cipher does every job: <b>AES-256-GCM</b>. There is no second AEAD in the
      product, so there is one nonce discipline, one tag check, and one implementation to audit.</p>
      <code>AES-256-GCM          blobs, cache, keystrokes, transport, ownership token
RSA-3072-OAEP        wraps the AES key for the ownership USB
P-256 ECDH + HKDF    fresh lookup keys; ML-KEM-768 joins at the handshake</code>

      <p>RSA and ECDH do not encrypt your content. They wrap or agree on AES keys. Every byte you
      actually care about is sealed with AES-256-GCM.</p>

      <blob id="b2" mime="text/plain"/>

      <h2>What this slice actually proves</h2>
      <p>This build is a vertical slice, not the finished browser. It proves the spine:
      a name resolves through the registry, a document loads and verifies its integrity, a
      PIN unlocks the profile, and a sealed blob opens only inside an unlocked session.</p>

      <quote>If the blob opens without the user key, the design is wrong. If it opens with the
      user key and leaves plaintext on disk, the implementation is wrong.</quote>

      <hr/>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>Real post-quantum recipient wrapping, the steganographic vault, mail and chat,
      Nickles, the arcade half of the home page, and the Linux shell. The order is in the
      roadmap, and the checklist gets a tick only when there is evidence.</p>

      <file id="manifest" mime="text/plain"/>

      <button intent="lock">Lock this session</button>
    </page>
  </body>
</kml>
